 \graphicspath{{../Figures/50-database/}{figs/50-database/}}
 
-I developed the Sensory Afferent Database (SADb), a curated literature resource for studying sensory feedback in locomotion and posture. It links findings from animal and human studies to afferent classes, sensory afferent pathways, and computational models. Researchers can use these records to compare individual feedback connections or whole neural circuits before implementing a neuromechanical model. This work forms part of the AARL's NeuroNex Communication, Coordination, and Control in Nervous Systems (C3NS) effort.
+Choosing a sensory connection for a locomotor controller requires more than the name of a receptor. A researcher needs to know which nerve was stimulated, which motor neurons responded, and whether the animal was at rest or moving. Those details are scattered across studies that used different species, preparations, and tasks. I developed the Sensory Afferent Database (SADb) to keep a proposed feedback pathway linked to the experiment that supports it. The collection also links to computational models so that a researcher can compare an experimental finding with the assumptions used to represent it. This work forms part of the AARL's NeuroNex Communication, Coordination, and Control in Nervous Systems (C3NS) effort.
 
 \noindent Presentation credits: The SADb resource was presented as an abstract and poster at Neuroscience 2023 \citep{bolen_sensory_2023}. The AI-assisted curation study was presented as an abstract and poster at Neuroscience 2024 \citep{bolen_using_2024}.
 
-SADb addresses the need to recover measured afferent properties, feedback pathways, and experimental conditions from a dispersed literature. A researcher performing neuromechanical modeling needs to know what was measured, how a pathway was stimulated or represented, and the locomotor or postural state in which it was studied. SADb brings individual studies into a searchable collection so that researchers can compare findings, locate evidence relevant to a candidate sensory afferent pathway, and follow each record to its source.
-
